 promotion = "Two seats, on different personas, each compiling its own instruments rather than reading the other's file. 265 wrote its derivation before `git fetch` and before any panel file but `INTENTS.md` and `OPTIONS.md` was opened, from the binding-time ruling and the admission rule, across six targets at three pointer widths (`265_probes/p1` through `p6`): a target-bound width builds where a literal of the same width does (`p1`), a const-bound width builds where a runtime-bound one is refused `E0015` (`p2a`/`p2b`), and one name pinned at one width disagrees with itself at another (`p3`). 260 derived the same reading from the format spine's storage rider and the locus clause's own establishing source, and compiled the discrimination from the other side: a value set fixed by a sibling datum in hand is refused `E0424`/`E0435`, the same set fixed by a `cfg`-selected const builds (`260_probes/p02`). Neither seat read the other before writing; 265's reconciliation, appended after `git fetch`, opened 260's committed probes as source and raw output rather than through its prose, and found the two routes discriminate the same binding-time line from opposite ends.\n\n**The one weakness in the count is named rather than hidden.** 260 read the registry's existing one-expert proposal (`proposal::each_choice_in_the_sequence_has_an_owner_and_a_resolution_time`) before forming its own view and says so, so on the bare headline sentence it is confirmation rather than a blind third instance, and 265 shares a persona with the proposal's own source. What is not confirmation is the mechanism: 260's compiled discrimination runs from the locus clause outward and needs nothing from the row it read, and it is that mechanism, not the sentence, the coordinator is gating on. Read that way the two seats independently derived the same boundary by two different routes, checked each other's raw output rather than each other's prose, and reached one disagreement across the reconciliation's own tally of agreements, which is resolved below by adopting the grammar `dimension.toml` actually permits.\n\n**The 64-bit ceiling is an artifact and not a wall**, on 260's instrument alone: the same affine predicate with its endpoints derived rather than declared builds at 64, at 100, at 126 and at the target's own width, and agrees with the shipped family at every one of its 62 admitted widths and both signednesses, with an agreement arm and a coverage arm both mutated and both firing. 265 accepts this by reading and built nothing at 64; the finding rests on 260's instrument alone and is recorded as such."
 because = "Under the settled ratification model the experts propose and the coordinator judges whether the reasoning, the evidence and the stated region are enough. Both seats compiled the same binding-time line from opposite starting points, with controls on every refusal, and neither result depends on reading the other's conclusion, which is what the mechanism-versus-sentence distinction in `promotion` is for. The reconciliation is unusually checkable: every agreement it lists names what each seat varied to reach it, the one disagreement in it is named and fixed rather than smoothed over, and its own author opened raw probe output rather than the other seat's prose before writing anything down."
 obligation = ["a_platform_sized_unsigned_integer_at_an_api_position"]
-note = "**Answering beyond the four recorded options is licensed by `ruling::the_option_set_is_not_a_boundary`**, which already lets the panel converge on a shape nobody wrote down; both seats independently found the four incomplete before either read the other.\n\n**The region, in the grammar `dimension.toml` admits**, which is 260's own table restated in the spelling 265's reconciliation corrects it to (`dimension::operation` and `dimension::strategy` both forbid the bare `any` 260 wrote, and `dimension::build_profile` and `dimension::toolchain` have no `any` form at all): within one compilation, `total_width: W in {16, 32}` established, `W = 64` refused by the shipped `Slots` ladder and not by this ruling; `fraction_width: F = 0`; `signedness: signedness in {unsigned, signed}`; `radix: radix = 2`; `phase: phase = 0`; `operation: membership and cardinality only, no arithmetic run`; `occupancy: occupancy any, by construction`; `threads: threads any, by construction`; `target_features: target features any, by construction`; `toolchain: rustc = 1.98.0-nightly (nightly-2026-05-28), edition = 2024`.\n\n**What the region wants and cannot have.** `dimension.toml` declares no axis indexing the compilation target's pointer width, so no predicate anywhere, this one included, can say \"holds at 64-bit targets\" in the registry's own notation. `260_probes/p04` counts it directly: 25 declared axes, 21 used, zero matching the target's width by name, against `target_features` and `container` as a positive control on the same census. Both seats independently name the pointer width as passing `dimension.toml`'s own test for an axis, on different personas, which is the header's own bar for a second reading; the declaration itself is not made here, because it was not this dispatch's question, and 265 leaves open whether the axis is the pointer width itself or the wider target triple it is one projection of, which a row keyed on the narrower spelling would then have to be worked around.\n\n**The 64-bit ceiling does not discharge the obligation this bears on.** `obligation::a_platform_sized_unsigned_integer_at_an_api_position` asks for a width a slot index carried in an `i64` cannot reach: that representation caps a declared width at 62, refused three ways at 64 bits, and the obligation's own errno and `GetLastError` ranges were measured to hold at 32 and fail at 16 within it. This ruling establishes that such a ceiling is a fact about one representation and not about what kind of thing a platform-width type is, which is what keeps it from reopening this question; naming the type is not delivering the obligation, and what discharges it is a host type wide enough for the target's pointer width, or a range restated as a count or an exclusive bound.\n\n**`Cap` is out of scope.** The question's own keywords carry it beside `USize`, and it names a different position, a const-generic capacity, which `ruling::the_numeric_door_carries_the_coordinate_set_and_the_two_type_bound_is_not_canon` already answers as a coordinate the door carries rather than a numeral. Nothing here touches it.\n\n**One finding travels with this and is not fixed here, because no round is open.** `arvo-format/src/slots.rs`'s own `E0277` diagnostic tells an outside implementor the trait is open and that what it owes is the `ADMITTED` obligation; `ADMITTED` then refuses every width past 62 unconditionally regardless of what an implementor declares, so the crate contradicts itself in two sentences about one trait."
